# tasks/

Your project's task ledger, provided by `rasa.module.tasks`. It is portable:
the same lifecycle serves a practice, a clinic, a manuscript and a workshop,
and it needs no version control, no network and no daemon to work.

The rules that govern every task live in **`.claude/task-rules.md`**. This
file is the map; that one is the law. Where they disagree, the law wins.

---

## The one idea to take away

**A task's state is the directory it is in. There is no `status:` field.**

The previous design stored the state twice -- once as a directory and once
as a frontmatter field -- and the two disagreed in more than half of all real
files, because moving a file *is* the act of changing its state and the
second write is the one people skip. The field is gone. The disagreement is
now unrepresentable rather than merely detectable.

The same principle runs through the whole schema: **one fact, one home**.
The title is the H1 and nowhere else. Whether a task is fully specified is
derived from whether it has acceptance criteria. Nothing that a human might
change is written down twice.

## The seven directories

```
intake.md -> triage/ -> backlog/ -> active/ -> review/ -> completed/
                            |          |         |
                            +------ blocked/ ----+
             (from anywhere) -------------------------> closed/
```

| Directory | What it means |
|---|---|
| `triage/` | Filed and has an id, but not agreed to yet. The only phase-less state. |
| `backlog/` | Phased and agreed, not started. |
| `active/` | In flight. |
| `review/` | The work is finished; the done-gate has not passed yet. |
| `blocked/` | Cannot proceed. Enterable from `backlog/`, `active/` or `review/`. Must carry a filled `## Blocker` section. |
| `completed/` | Terminal, and good: the done-gate passed. |
| `closed/` | Terminal, and not done: superseded, duplicated, overtaken, or decided against. Must carry a `resolution:`. |

Two of those are worth dwelling on.

**`review/` is where the done-gate runs.** Without it, finished-but-unchecked
work has nowhere to sit, so it stays in `active/` and `active/` stops meaning
anything. It is also the most domain-native state in the set: a partner's
review, a clinical sign-off and a cooling-off re-read are all literally this.

**`closed/` keeps the count honest.** `completed/` is the number everybody
reads. Folding abandoned and superseded work into it makes that number a lie.
A successful task therefore carries no `resolution:` field at all -- the
directory already says it succeeded.

## Moving a task

Use the shipped command, never a bare move. One command writes the
conditional frontmatter, appends the history line, and moves the file --
together, so they cannot come apart:

```
task new --type <type> "<title>"
task graduate <id> --phase <phase>    task demote <id>
task start <id>                       task park <id>
task submit <id>                      task reject <id> --note "<gate that failed>"
task block <id>                       task unblock <id>
task pass <id> --by <handle>
task close <id> --resolution <r> --by <handle> [--ref <id>]
task reopen <id> --to <stage> --note "<why>"
```

Moving a file by hand does not corrupt anything -- the tree stays valid --
but the history will not know about it. The validator detects that and
`check-tasks --fix` reconciles it, dated today, attributed to `unknown`. It
never invents a past date.

## What is in a task file

Five keys are always required, and four of the five are stamped by the
tooling. Filing a task costs you one typed value and a title.

| Key | Required | Who writes it |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | always | the allocator, once, immutable |
| `type` | always | you: `change`, `defect`, `upkeep`, `inquiry` or `record` |
| `created` | always | the allocator, once, never rewritten |
| `created_by` | always | the allocator, once, never rewritten |
| `updated` | always | every lifecycle command |
| `phase` | outside `triage/` and `closed/` | you, when the task graduates |
| `target` | iff `targets` is declared | you |
| `completed_by` | in `completed/` and `closed/` | `task pass` / `task close` |
| `resolution` | in `closed/` only | `task close` |
| `resolution_ref` | iff superseded or duplicate | `task close` |
| `priority` | optional | you: `now`, `high`, `normal` (default) or `low` |
| `needs` | optional | you: ids that must reach `completed/` first |
| `x-<anything>` | optional | you: preserved, never interpreted |

Any other bare key is an error. That is deliberate: it is what stops the
frontmatter sprawling into forty invented keys again. When you genuinely
need a field of your own, prefix it with `x-` and it is yours forever.

**`updated` is checked, not trusted.** A content digest is kept per task; if
a file's contents change while its `updated` date does not, that is a hard
error, not a shrug. No timestamps from the filesystem and no version control
are consulted anywhere in that check.

**Depth is derived.** A task is *stub depth* when it has no
`## Acceptance criteria` heading or that heading holds no real checkbox, and
*spec depth* otherwise. There is no depth field and no marker string to keep
in step. Templates for both depths and all five types are in
`.claude/task-templates/` -- start with its `README.md`.

## The other files in here

| File | Owner | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `intake.md` | you | Pre-triage capture. One bullet, no id, no obligation. |
| `ROADMAP.md` | you | The phase registry. Every phase and the tasks under it. |
| `AUDIT.md` | you | The narrative ledger: what happened, when, and why it mattered. |
| `tasks.config.yml` | you | Your declarations: actors, targets, series, the ratchet date. Optional. |
| `history.tsv` | the tooling | Append-only transition log. Never hand-edit it. |
| `.state/` | the tooling | Caches and the allocation lock. Regenerable; ignore it. |

### `history.tsv`

Six tab-separated columns, a header line, then one line per transition,
oldest first:

```
date    id    from    to    actor    note
```

- `date` -- `YYYY-MM-DD`. Dates never go backwards down the file.
- `id` -- the task.
- `from` -- the stage it left, or `-` for the line that files it.
- `to` -- the stage it entered. One of the seven directory names.
- `actor` -- a handle, or `unknown`.
- `note` -- free text. May be empty. This is where a `pass` records which
  gate was satisfied and a `reject` records which one was not.

It is four things at once: the transition log, the audit trail a project
without version control has never had, the corroboration for `created`,
`created_by` and `completed_by`, and -- because it is append-only -- the
retirement ledger that stops a deleted task's number from ever being handed
out again. Do not sort it, do not tidy it, do not delete lines from it.

### `intake` and `triage` are not the same thing

`intake.md` costs one line and buys nothing but a record that you thought of
it. `triage/` costs an id and a file. Keep the cheap layer cheap: put
half-ideas and complaints in `intake.md`, and promote one into `triage/` only
when you have decided it is real. Both layers rot if nobody sweeps them, so
sweep them on a cadence you can actually keep.

## The done-gate

A task reaches `completed/` only when both hold:

1. every acceptance criterion is met **and actually verified**, and
2. the project's done-gate passes.

What "verified" requires is the one thing that varies by project, so it is
not written down anywhere in this module. It lives in
**`.claude/done-gate.md`**, which is yours. Fill it with gates that are
objectively checkable -- something that succeeds or fails, a named person's
approval, a document that now exists -- and never step around one to finish
faster. Stepping around it is the whole failure this lifecycle exists to make
visible.

